{"reason": "The current model overfits quickly (best epoch at 18, initial val loss 0.1432 vs train 0.0799), uses only simple Morgan/MACCS features without leveraging powerful pre-trained molecular embeddings, and lacks robust handling of high missing rates (e.g., MGMB 96% missing). Adding graph-based pretrained embeddings (ChemBERTa-2) and uncertainty weighting per task will improve generalization and handle sparse targets better.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Change #1: Category: Feature Engineering. What: Add ChemBERTa-2 embeddings (768-dim) as additional input features. Use the 'DeepChem/ChemBERTa-77M-MLM' model from HuggingFace to extract [CLS] token embeddings for each SMILES. Why: Pretrained molecular transformers capture rich semantic and structural information that hand-crafted fingerprints miss, providing better inductive bias for ADMET prediction. Implementation: Load tokenizer and model (with trust_remote_code=True), batch-generate embeddings for all molecules (train+test), concatenate with existing RDKit features, and standardize. This increases feature dimension but adds informative signal.", "model_design": "Change #2: Category: Model Architecture. What: Add uncertainty-weighted multi-task learning with task-specific attention. Introduce a learned task uncertainty parameter (log variance) per output head, and use negative log-likelihood loss (Gaussian) to dynamically weight tasks. Also add a cross-task attention mechanism that allows tasks to share information. Why: High missingness (e.g., MGMB 96%) requires adaptive weighting; tasks with more data should influence training more. The attention mechanism helps propagate knowledge from well-measured tasks to sparse ones. Implementation: Replace heads with uncertainty-aware outputs (mean + log_var), modify loss to NLL, and add a small transformer encoder after shared features for cross-task attention.", "training_evaluation": "Change #3: Category: Training Strategy. What: Implement progressive unfreezing (train only new heads first, then whole model) and use cosine annealing with warm restarts, but with a longer first cycle (T_0=50) and lower eta_min (1e-6). Add MixUp augmentation on features to regularize. Why: The model's rapid overfitting (best epoch 18) suggests need for stronger regularization. Progressive unfreezing prevents catastrophic forgetting of pretrained embeddings, MixUp improves generalization, and longer cycles allow better convergence. Implementation: Freeze ChemBERTa backbone for first 20 epochs, then unfreeze. Use Beta(2,2) distributed MixUp on feature batches. Keep early stopping with patience=50 and monitor validation MA-RAE."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The current model overfits quickly (best epoch at 18, initial val loss 0.1432 vs train 0.0799), uses only simple Morgan/MACCS features without leveraging powerful pre-trained molecular embeddings, and lacks robust handling of high missing rates (e.g., MGMB 96% missing). Adding graph-based pretrained embeddings (ChemBERTa-2) and uncertainty weighting per task will improve generalization and handle sparse targets better.",
